1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,850
Clare: Hi Jake. How are you getting on with the practical teaching?

2
00:00:03,850 --> 00:00:08,700
Jake: It's harder than I expected, but I've got some great classes. How about you?

3
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:13,850
Clare: Not brilliant. I'm really struggling with my Year 12 science class.

4
00:00:13,850 --> 00:00:15,630
Jake: Are they hard to control?

5
00:00:15,630 --> 00:00:18,561
Clare: Well, I don't have discipline problems as such.

6
00:00:18,561 --> 00:00:25,010
It's just that they don't seem to think that science has anything to do with their lives. It's depressing.

7
00:00:25,010 --> 00:00:26,935
Clare: They listen to what I say,

8
00:00:26,935 --> 00:00:30,360
and I gave them a test last week and the results weren't too bad

9
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:32,710
but there's no real engagement.

10
00:00:32,710 --> 00:00:34,110
Jake: Right.

11
00:00:34,110 --> 00:00:36,020
Clare: And as part of my teaching practice

12
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:39,610
I have to design an experiment for them to do.

13
00:00:39,610 --> 00:00:42,542
Clare: I was wondering about something on the children's diets.

14
00:00:42,542 --> 00:00:45,060
you know, asking them to record what they eat

15
00:00:45,060 --> 00:00:47,340
and maybe linking it to their state of health.

16
00:00:47,340 --> 00:00:54,570
Jake: Mmm. Let's think. So your methodology would involve the children recording what they eat.

17
00:00:54,570 --> 00:01:00,345
Jake: OK, but you'd also need to have access to the children's medical records

18
00:01:00,345 --> 00:01:03,020
and I don't think people would be happy about that

19
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:06,120
confidentiality would be an issue.

20
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:08,645
Jake: If you could get the right data,

21
00:01:08,645 --> 00:01:13,380
the conclusions might be significant, but I suspect it's not going to be easy.

22
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:13,930
Clare: Right.

23
00:01:13,930 --> 00:01:17,660
Jake: Have you thought about doing an experiment using animals?

24
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:20,240
Clare: Wouldn't that be upsetting for the children?

25
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:24,304
Jake: Well, the animals don't have to be harmed in any way.

26
00:01:24,304 --> 00:01:25,720
It could just be an experiment

27
00:01:25,720 --> 00:01:29,930
where they're given a certain diet and the effects are observed.

28
00:01:29,930 --> 00:01:32,690
Clare: Would I have to get permission to use animals?

29
00:01:32,690 --> 00:01:38,833
Jake: Yes, you'd have to submit an outline of the experiment and fill in a form,

30
00:01:38,833 --> 00:01:40,640
but it's quite straightforward.

31
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:42,540
Clare: But if we found out that, say

32
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:45,457
a particular diet affects the health of animals,

33
00:01:45,457 --> 00:01:49,070
the same thing wouldn't necessarily be true for people, would it?

34
00:01:49,070 --> 00:01:55,380
Jake: No that's true, but the findings for any experiment are going to be limited. It's inevitable.

35
00:01:55,380 --> 00:02:02,860
Clare: I suppose so. So what animals could I use to investigate the effects of diet? Mice?

36
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:05,980
Jake: Yes. You'd need experimental mice

37
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:09,240
ones that have been specially bred for experiments.

38
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:14,010
Jake: OK, so what will your experiment be investigating exactly?

39
00:02:14,010 --> 00:02:16,720
Clare: Well, something to do with nutrition

40
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:19,716
So maybe we could look at food supplements.

41
00:02:19,716 --> 00:02:24,280
things like extra iron and extra protein, and their impact on health.

42
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:26,978
Jake: Mmm. That might be rather broad.

43
00:02:26,978 --> 00:02:30,700
Maybe just look at the effects of one supplement

44
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:33,530
like sugar, on the health of the mice?

45
00:02:33,530 --> 00:02:36,540
Clare: In fact, maybe the focus could be on

46
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:38,960
whether mice can control their own diet....

47
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:42,060
Jake: So, what happens when they have access to more sugar

48
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:43,730
that they don't really need?

49
00:02:43,730 --> 00:02:47,890
Clare: Exactly. Do they eat it or do they decide to leave it?

50
00:02:47,890 --> 00:02:54,150
Jake: Great. Then later on, you could do a follow-up experiment adding another variable.

51
00:02:54,150 --> 00:02:58,800
Jake: Like, you could give some of the mice the chance to be more active,

52
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:04,070
running on a wheel or something, and the others just sit around and don't do much.

53
00:03:04,070 --> 00:03:09,737
Clare: Or I could repeat the experiment but change the type of food I provided.

54
00:03:09,737 --> 00:03:12,760
or use mice with a different genetic structure.

55
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:15,520
Clare: But I think your idea would be more interesting

56
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:19,550
I might think about that some more.

57
00:03:19,550 --> 00:03:22,140
Clare: So can I talk through a possible procedure for the experiment

58
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:24,640
where mice are given a sugar supplement?

59
00:03:24,640 --> 00:03:28,110
Jake: Sure. I did a similar experiment in college actually.

60
00:03:28,110 --> 00:03:31,650
Clare: Great. So how many mice would I need?

61
00:03:31,650 --> 00:03:37,630
Jake: I'd say about 12. And all young ones, not a mixture of old and young.

62
00:03:37,630 --> 00:03:44,220
Clare: OK. And I'd need two groups of equal sizes, so six in each group

63
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:46,910
And how would I tell them apart?

64
00:03:46,910 --> 00:03:50,860
Clare: I suppose I could put some sort of tag on one group

65
00:03:50,860 --> 00:03:53,280
or just mark them in some way?

66
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:56,160
Jake: You could use food colouring, that wouldn't hurt them.

67
00:03:56,160 --> 00:04:00,270
Clare: Perfect. Then each group would go into a separate cage,

68
00:04:00,270 --> 00:04:05,500
and one group, let's call them group A, would be the control group.

69
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:09,730
Clare: So they'd just have ordinary mouse food. I suppose you can buy that?

70
00:04:09,730 --> 00:04:12,110
Jake: Yes, it comes in dry pellets.

71
00:04:12,110 --> 00:04:14,980
Clare: And the other group would have the same as the first group

72
00:04:14,980 --> 00:04:17,520
but they'd also have the extra sugar.

73
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:19,860
Jake: Would you just give them straight sugar?

74
00:04:19,860 --> 00:04:23,140
Clare: It might be better to give them something like cereal with it.

75
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:28,608
Jake: Mmm. Then you'd need to weigh the mice, I should think once a week.

76
00:04:28,608 --> 00:04:31,000
And you'd need an electronic balance.

77
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:35,010
Clare: But we can't hold them on the balance, or it'd affect the reading.

78
00:04:35,010 --> 00:04:41,530
Jake: Exactly. So you need something called a weighing chamber to stop the mice from running away.

79
00:04:41,530 --> 00:04:47,690
Jake: It sounds complicated, but actually you can just use a plastic box with holes in the top.

80
00:04:47,690 --> 00:04:55,247
Clare: OK. So once we've measured the weight gain of each mouse we can work out the average for each group,

81
00:04:55,247 --> 00:04:57,307
as well as the standard deviation.

82
00:04:57,307 --> 00:05:01,060
And then see where we go from there. That sounds cool

83
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:03,490
I think the students will enjoy it.

84
00:05:03,490 --> 00:05:06,090
Jake: Yes. One thing you must remember...
